Define $R_{ij}\defeq a_i \abs{ raket{a_i}{g_j}}^2$ and $R'_{ij}\defeq g_i \abs{ raket{a_i}{g_j}}^2$. Note that both $R \defeq \{R_{ij}\}$ and $R' \defeq \{R'_{ij}\}$ form probability distributions over $[N^2]$. Also note that $\F(R,R') = \Tr(\sqrt{ho}\sqrt{ho'})$. Consider  egin{align*} \abs{ raket{S_A(ho)}{S_A(ho')}} & =\sum_{i,j}\min(R_{ij},R'_{i,j}) = 1 -  rac{1}{2}\onenorm{R-R'} 
onumber\ & \geq 1 - \sqrt{1-\F(R,R')^2} = 1 - \sqrt{1-(\Tr \sqrt{ho}\sqrt{ho'})^2} 
onumber \ & \geq 1 - \sqrt{2(1- \Tr \sqrt{ho}\sqrt{ho'})} \geq 1- \sqrt{2 \sqrt{1-\F(ho,ho')^2}} 
onumber \ & \geq 1- 2 (1-\F(ho,ho'))^{1/4} . \myqedhere \end{align*} where the first equality is from the definitions of $\ket{S_A(ho)}$ and $\ket{S_A(ho')}$; the second equality is from the definition of $\ell_1$ distance; the first inequality is from ~ef{fact:tracefidelityequi}; the second inequality is from the fact that $\Tr\sqrt{ho}\sqrt{ho'}\leq 1$; the third inequality is from Facts~ef{fact:fidelityvstrace}.

Let the eigen-decomposition of $ho$ be $\sum_{i=1}^Na_i\ketbra{a_i}$ and that of $\sigma$ be $\sum_{i=1}^Nb_i\ketbra{b_i}$. Define $c\defeqelent{ho}{\sigma}$, $\delta \defeq (\ve/3)^4$ and $c' \defeq (c+2)/\delta$. Without loss of generality, assume $a_1,a_2\ldots a_N,  rac{2^{c'}}{\delta}b_1, rac{2^{c'}}{\delta}b_2\ldots  rac{2^{c'}}{\delta}b_N$ to be rational numbers, and define $K$ be the least common multiple of their denominators. The error due to this assumption can be made arbitrarily close to $0$, for large enough $K$. Let $\set{\ket{1},\ket{2}\ldots \ket{N}}$ be an orthonormal basis for $\H$. Introduce registers $A_1,B_1$ associated to $\H$ and registers $A_2,B_2$ associated to some Hilbert space $\H'$ with an orthonormal basis $\set{\ket{1},\ket{2}\ldots \ket{K}}$. Consider the following state on $A_1,A_2,B_1,B_2$.  egin{equation} \ket{S}_{A_1A_2B_1B_2} \defeq  rac{1}{\sqrt{KN}}\sum_{i=1}^N \ket{i,i}_{A_1B_1} \otimes \left( \sum_{m=1}^{K}\ket{m, m}_{A_2B_2} ight) \end{equation} For brevity, define registers $A,B$ such that $A\defeq A_1A_2$ and $B\defeq B_1B_2$. The protocol is described below.  egin{mdframed}  igskip 	extbf{Input:} Alice is given $ho=\sum_{i=1}^Na_i\ketbra{a_i}$. Bob is given $\sigma=\sum_{i=1}^Nb_i\ketbra{b_i}$.  igskip 	extbf{Shared resources:} Alice and Bob hold $\lceil N\log( rac{1}{\delta}) ceil$ registers $A_1^iA_2^iB_1^iB_2^i$ ($i \in [\lceil N\log( rac{1}{\delta}) ceil]$), such that $A_1^i\equiv A_1, A^i_2\equiv A_2, B_1^i\equiv B_1,B_2^i\equiv B_2$. The shared state in register $A_1^iA_2^iB_1^iB_2^i$ is $\ket{S}_{A_1^iA_2^iB_1^iB_2^i}$. Let $i$ refer to the `index' of corresponding registers. They also share infinitely many random hash functions $h_1,h_2,\cdots$, where each $h_l:\set{0,\cdots,N-1}ightarrow\set{0,1}$.  egin{enumerate} \item 	extbf{For} $i=1$ to $\lceil N\log( rac{1}{\delta}) ceil$,  egin{enumerate} \item Alice performs the measurement $\set{P_A,I_A- P_A}$ on each register $A_1^iA^i_2$ where,  egin{equation} P_A\defeq\sum_i\ket{a_i}_{A_1} ra{a_i}_{A_1}\otimes\left(\sum_{m=1}^{Ka_i}\ket{m}_{A_2} ra{m}_{A_2}ight) . \end{equation} On each index $i$, she declares 	extit{success} if her outcome corresponds to $P_A$. \item Bob performs the measurement $\set{P_B,I_B- P_B}$ on each register $B_1^iB^i_2$ where,  egin{equation} P_B\defeq \sum_i\ket{b_i}_{B_1} ra{b_i}_{B_1}\otimes\left(\sum_{m=1}^{\min\{ rac{K}{\delta} 2^{c'} b_i,K\}}\ket{m}_{B_2} ra{m}_{B_2}ight) . \end{equation} On each index $i$, he declares 	extit{success} if his outcome corresponds to $P_B$. \end{enumerate} 	extbf{Endfor} \item If Alice does not succeed on any index, she aborts. \item Else, Alice selects the first index $m$ where she succeeds and sends to Bob the binary encoding of $k=\lceil m/Nceil$ using $\lceil\log\log rac{1}{\delta}ceil$ bits. \item Alice sends $\{ h_l(m\mod N) |~ l \in [\lceil r + 2\log rac{1}{\delta}ceil]\}$ to Bob. \item Define $S_B \defeq \{ t |~ 	ext{ Bob succeeds on index } $t$\} \cap \{(k-1)N, \cdots, k N -1\}$. If $S_B$ is empty, he outputs $\ketbra{0}$. Bob selects the first index $n$ in $S_B$ such that $ orall l \in [\lceil r + 2\log rac{1}{\delta}ceil]: h_l(n \mod N) = h_l(m \mod N) $ and outputs the state in $B_1^n$ (if no such index exists, he outputs $\ketbra{0}$). \end{enumerate} \end{mdframed}  igskip We analyze the protocol through a series of claims. Following claim computes the probability of success for Alice and Bob.  egin{claim} For each index $i$, $\prob{	ext{Alice succeeds}}= rac{1}{N}$; $\prob{	ext{Bob succeeds}}\leq rac{2^{c'}}{\delta N}$ \end{claim}  egin{proof} Follows from direct calculation. \end{proof} From quantum substrate theorem (Fact ef{fact:substrate}), there exists a state $ho'$ which satisfies $\F(ho,ho')\geq 1-\delta$ and $$minent{ho'}{\sigma}\leq rac{elent{ho}{\sigma}+1}{\delta}+\log rac{1}{1-\delta}\leq  rac{elent{ho}{\sigma}+2}{\delta}=c'.$$ We prove the following claim which is of independent interest as well.  egin{claim} Let $ho'$ have the eigen-decomposition $ho' = \sum_i g_i\ket{g_i} ra{g_i}$. For any $p>0$ and every $\ket{g_i} ra{g_i}$, we have $\sum_{j|~b_j\leq p\cdot g_i}\abs{ raket{b_j}{g_i}}^2 \leq 2^{c'}\cdot p$. \end{claim}  egin{proof} Since $ho' \leq 2^{c'}\sigma$, it implies $g_i\ket{g_i} ra{g_i} \leq 2^{c'}\sigma$. Let $\Pi$ be the projection onto the eigen-space of $\sigma$ with eigenvalues less than or equal to $p\cdot g_i$. We have $\Pi\sigma\Pi \leq p\cdot g_i\cdot\Pi$. After applying $\Pi$ on both sides of the equation $g_i\ket{g_i} ra{g_i} \leq 2^{c'}\sigma$ and taking operator norm on both sides, we get $g_i\sum_{j:~b_j \leq p\cdot g_i}\abs{ raket{b_j}{g_i}}^2 \leq 2^{c'}\cdot p \cdot g_i $. This implies the lemma. \end{proof} Define $$\ket{S_A(ho)}\defeq  rac{1}{\sqrt{K}}\sum_{i=1}^N\ket{a_i}\ket{\conjugate{a_i}}\otimes \left(\sum_{m=1}^{Ka_i}\ket{m, m}ight) ; $$ $$\ket{S_A(ho')}\defeq rac{1}{\sqrt{K}}\sum_{i=1}^N\ket{g_i}\ket{\conjugate{g_i}}\otimes \left(\sum_{m=1}^{\lceil Kg_i ceil}\ket{m, m} ight) .$$ Here $\ket{\conjugate{a_i}}$ (similarly $\ket{\conjugate{g_i}}$) is the state obtained by taking complex conjugate of $\ket{a_i}$ ($\ket{g_i}$), with respect to the basis $\set{\ket{1},\ket{2}\ldots \ket{N}}$ in $\H$. The following claim asserts that $\ket{S_A(ho)}$ and $\ket{S_A(ho')}$ are close if $ho$ and $ho'$ are close.  egin{claim} $\abs{ raket{S_A(ho)}{S_A(ho')}} \geq 1- 2 (1-\F(ho,ho'))^{1/4} .$ \end{claim}  egin{proof} Define $R_{ij}\defeq a_i \abs{ raket{a_i}{g_j}}^2$ and $R'_{ij}\defeq g_i \abs{ raket{a_i}{g_j}}^2$. Note that both $R \defeq \{R_{ij}\}$ and $R' \defeq \{R'_{ij}\}$ form probability distributions over $[N^2]$. Also note that $\F(R,R') = \Tr(\sqrt{ho}\sqrt{ho'})$. Consider  egin{align*} \abs{ raket{S_A(ho)}{S_A(ho')}} & =\sum_{i,j}\min(R_{ij},R'_{i,j}) = 1 -  rac{1}{2}\onenorm{R-R'} 
onumber\ & \geq 1 - \sqrt{1-\F(R,R')^2} = 1 - \sqrt{1-(\Tr \sqrt{ho}\sqrt{ho'})^2} 
onumber \ & \geq 1 - \sqrt{2(1- \Tr \sqrt{ho}\sqrt{ho'})} \geq 1- \sqrt{2 \sqrt{1-\F(ho,ho')^2}} 
onumber \ & \geq 1- 2 (1-\F(ho,ho'))^{1/4} . \myqedhere \end{align*} where the first equality is from the definitions of $\ket{S_A(ho)}$ and $\ket{S_A(ho')}$; the second equality is from the definition of $\ell_1$ distance; the first inequality is from ~ef{fact:tracefidelityequi}; the second inequality is from the fact that $\Tr\sqrt{ho}\sqrt{ho'}\leq 1$; the third inequality is from Facts~ef{fact:fidelityvstrace}. \end{proof} We use these claims to prove the following.  egin{claim} For each index $i$, $\prob{	ext{Bob succeeds}|~	ext{Alice succeeds}}\geq 1 - \delta - 2 \delta^{1/4} \geq 1 - \ve $. \end{claim}  egin{proof} Consider,  egin{equation*} (I_A\otimes P_B)\ket{S_A(ho')} =  rac{1}{\sqrt{K}}\sum_{i,j=1}^N\ket{\conjugate{g_j}} \ket{b_i} raket{b_i}{g_j} \left(\sum_{m=1}^{\min\{\lceil Kg_jceil,  rac{K}{\delta} 2^{c'}b_i\}}\ket{m, m} ight). \end{equation*} Therefore,  egin{align} & 
orm{(I_A\otimes P_B)\ket{S_A(ho')} }^2 \geq \sum_{i,j=1}^N \abs{ raket{b_i}{g_j}}^2 \min\{g_j,  rac{1}{\delta}2^{c'}b_i \} 
onumber \ & \geq \sum_{j=1}^N g_j \left(\sum_{i|~b_i\geq \delta 2^{-c'}g_j} \abs{ raket{b_i}{g_j}}^2 ight) \geq \sum_{j=1}^N g_j(1- \delta) = 1- \delta . \quad \mbox{(using Claim~ef{claim:1})} \end{align} Using the above,  egin{align*} & \prob{	ext{Bob succeeds}|~	ext{Alice succeeds}} = \Tr (I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho)} \ & \geq \Tr (I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho')} -  rac{1}{2} \onenorm{S_A(ho) - S_A(ho')} \ & = \Tr (I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho')} - \sqrt{1- \abs{ raket{S_A(ho)}{S_A(ho')}}^2} \quad \mbox{(Fact~ef{fact:tracefidelityequi})} \ & \geq 1 - \delta - 2 \sqrt{ (1-\F(ho,ho'))^{1/2}} . \quad \mbox{(Claim~ef{claim:2} and Eq.~\eqref{eq:probonrho'})} \end{align*} \end{proof} Finally, we show that if Alice and Bob succeed together on an index, the state in register $B$ with Bob is close to $ho$.  egin{claim} Given that both Alice and Bob succeed, fidelity between $ho$ and the state of the register $B$ is at least $\sqrt{1 - \delta - 2 \delta^{1/4}} \geq 1 - \ve $ . \end{claim}  egin{proof} From gentle measurement lemma (Fact~ef{fact:gentlemeasurement}), $$\F(S_A(ho),  rac{(I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho)} (I_A\otimes P_B) }{\Tr (I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho)}}) \geq \sqrt{\Tr (I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho)}}. $$ Since the marginal of $\ket{S_A(ho)}$ on register $B$ is $ho$ and partial trace does not decrease fidelity (Fact~ef{fact:monotonequantumoperation}), using item 2. above, the desired result follows. \end{proof} Let $j$ be the first index where Alice and Bob both succeed. As described in the protocol, $m$ is the first index where Alice succeeds and $n$ is the index such that Bob outputs the state in $B_1^n$. We have the following claim,  egin{claim} With probability at least $1-4\ve$, $m=n=j$. \end{claim} Before proving Claim~ef{claim:highprobindex}, let us define the following "bad" events.  egin{definition}  egin{itemize} \item $T_1$ is the event that Alice does not succeed on any of the indices. \item $T_2$ is the event that $m 
